  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Sam Covelli is an American restaurant operator and owner of Covelli Enterprises, based in Warren, Ohio. He succeeded his father, Albert, in the family business and redirected its McDonald’s franchise portfolio towards Panera Bread. A University of Miami business graduate, he expanded the group into one of Panera’s largest franchise operators.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 94,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Company history, trade reporting and a regional biography establish the concise identity."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S2",
      "S4"
    ],
    "word_count": 51,
    "html": "<p>Sam Covelli is an American restaurant operator and owner of Covelli Enterprises, based in Warren, Ohio. He succeeded his father, Albert, in the family business and redirected its McDonald’s franchise portfolio towards Panera Bread. A University of Miami business graduate, he expanded the group into one of Panera’s largest franchise operators.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Before opening a restaurant in Elyria, Sam Covelli inspected details from its sign to the artwork on its walls. He asked for larger versions of both and checked that they would arrive before the opening. Even a leaf blown through the entrance caught his attention: he bent down to pick it up during the visit.\n\nHis approach to food donations also became part of daily operations. Covelli instructed bakers to prepare more than they expected to sell, reserving the surplus for local organisations. Regional marketing staff worked with community groups to arrange help, while unsold bread and bakery products went to food banks and hunger-relief agencies.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 92,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Reported first-hand inspection and separately corroborated food-donation operations support the distinct dimensions."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S3",
      "S2"
    ],
    "word_count": 106,
    "html": "<p>Before opening a restaurant in Elyria, Sam Covelli inspected details from its sign to the artwork on its walls. He asked for larger versions of both and checked that they would arrive before the opening. Even a leaf blown through the entrance caught his attention: he bent down to pick it up during the visit.</p>\r\n<p>His approach to food donations also became part of daily operations. Covelli instructed bakers to prepare more than they expected to sell, reserving the surplus for local organisations. Regional marketing staff worked with community groups to arrange help, while unsold bread and bakery products went to food banks and hunger-relief agencies.</p>\r\n"
  },
